backendImplications:
Schemas (schemas/): use a discriminated union for the change objects:
- task_done {project_id, task_id}
- task_add / scope_add {project_id, text ≤120, hours 0.25..80}
- blocker / note {project_id, text ≤160}
- confidence {project_id, value int 1..5}
- target_move {project_id, date ISO}
- hours_per_day {project_id, value (0,8]}
- bau_done {routine_id}
The envelope is {summary: str, changes: Change[], unplaced: str[≤4]}. Prefer structured output / tool-use JSON over the greedy regex.

risksAndQuirks:
applyCheckIn is called for EVERY project with any accepted change, even a target move alone. So it always:
- writes a check-in record ('Plan confirmed.' if there is no text), updates the checkin date and drops the stale feed item;
- sets blocker = d.blockers || null, silently CLEARING an existing blocker;
- keeps only the FIRST blocker when there are several.
When hours/day moved the forecast without scope, the feed says 'Forecast holds at <new date>', which is misleading.
